<div class="section-label">Full study-guide coverage from attached HTML</div>
<div class="legacy-content"><h3 class="subsection">Methods &amp; Math</h3>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Lorentz Transforms</h4>
<p class="gloss">Maps an event's coordinates between two inertial frames moving at relative velocity $v$ along $x$, preserving the speed of light and mixing space and time.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Boost along x</span>
      $$ x' = \gamma(x - vt), \qquad t' = \gamma\!\left(t - \tfrac{vx}{c^2}\right), \qquad y' = y,\ z' = z $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Lorentz factor</span>
      $$ \gamma = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}} \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; \beta \equiv v/c $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Inverse (swap sign of $v$)</span>
      $$ x = \gamma(x' + vt'), \qquad t = \gamma\!\left(t' + \tfrac{vx'}{c^2}\right) $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Minkowski Spacetime Diagrams</h4>
<p class="gloss">Plot $ct$ vs $x$ with light-cone lines at $45°$. A boosted frame's axes <em class="term">scissor inward</em> toward the light cone — the $x'$-axis tilts up and the $ct'$-axis tilts right by the same angle $\theta$ where $\tan\theta = v/c$.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Axes of frame $S'$ in $S$</span>
      $$ \text{$ct'$-axis: } x = \beta\,(ct), \qquad \text{$x'$-axis: } ct = \beta\,x $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Lines of simultaneity</span>
      $$ t' = \text{const} \;\Leftrightarrow\; ct = \beta\,x + \text{const} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Lorentz Velocity Transforms</h4>
<p class="gloss">Velocities don't add linearly; the law guarantees nothing reaches $c$ from below.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Parallel and perpendicular components</span>
      $$ u'_\parallel = \frac{u_\parallel - v}{1 - u_\parallel v/c^2}, \qquad u'_\perp = \frac{u_\perp}{\gamma(1 - u_\parallel v/c^2)} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Photon check</span>
      $$ u_\parallel = c \;\Longrightarrow\; u'_\parallel = c \quad \text{(any $v$)} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Relativistic Invariants</h4>
<p class="gloss">Quantities that all inertial observers agree on. The mass-energy invariant defines rest mass; proper time is the time elapsed on a clock carried along a worldline.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Mass–energy relation</span>
      $$ E^2 = (pc)^2 + (mc^2)^2, \qquad E = \gamma mc^2,\ \ \mathbf{p} = \gamma m\mathbf{v} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Spacetime interval &amp; proper time</span>
      $$ \Delta s^2 = -(c\Delta t)^2 + \Delta x^2,\qquad d\tau = \frac{dt}{\gamma} = dt\sqrt{1-\beta^2} $$
    </div>
</div>
<h3 class="subsection">Concepts</h3>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Why space, time, and speed are relative</h4>
<p>Einstein argued from two postulates: physics looks the same in every inertial frame, and the speed of light is universal. Two observers cannot agree on the timing or distance of separated events without disagreeing on $c$ — so space and time themselves must be frame-dependent.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Events and the relativity of simultaneity</h4>
<p>An <em class="term">event</em> is a point $(t,\mathbf{x})$ in spacetime. Two events at the same $t$ in frame $S$ are generally <em class="term">not</em> simultaneous in $S'$: simultaneity is a frame-dependent slicing of spacetime.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Resolving the classical paradoxes</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Twin paradox.</strong> The traveling twin's worldline isn't inertial — turning around breaks the symmetry, so they age less.</li>
<li><strong>Flashlight on a moving spaceship.</strong> Light still travels at $c$ in every frame; only the wavelength shifts (Doppler).</li>
<li><strong>Ladder in the barn.</strong> The two doors are simultaneously closed in the barn frame but not the ladder frame — both descriptions are self-consistent because simultaneity is relative.</li>
</ul>
</div></div>
</section>
