<section class="double-glass">
<div class="section-label">Conceptual moves</div>
<ul class="concept-list"><li>Continuity of ψ and dψ/dx at finite interfaces is the fastest way to set up reflection/transmission problems.</li><li>Measurement probabilities come from projection amplitude squared, not from the raw amplitude.</li><li>Spin is not a tiny planet orbit; it is an intrinsic two-state angular momentum with projection ±ℏ/2.</li><li>Bell/EPR rules out local hidden variables, not every realist interpretation; Bohmian mechanics survives by being nonlocal.</li><li>Bands come from many wells coupled together: bonding/antibonding split into nearly continuous allowed bands.</li></ul>
</section>
<section class="double-glass legacy-shell">
<div class="section-label">Full study-guide coverage from attached HTML</div>
<div class="legacy-content"><h3 class="subsection">Methods &amp; Math</h3>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Plane Wave (Complex Exponential)</h4>
<p class="gloss">The fundamental harmonic solution; everything else is built from superposing these.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \psi(x,t) = A\,e^{i(kx - \omega t)} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ k = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda},\qquad \omega = 2\pi f $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>de Broglie Relations</h4>
<p class="gloss">Identify particle energy and momentum with the wave's frequency and wavenumber.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ E = \hbar\omega = hf, \qquad p = \hbar k = \frac{h}{\lambda} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Schrödinger Equation</h4>
<p class="gloss">Energy comes from the time derivative; momentum from the spatial derivative — promoting de Broglie's identifications to operator equations.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Operator identifications</span>
      $$ \hat{E} = i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial t},\qquad \hat{p} = -i\hbar\frac{\partial}{\partial x} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Time-dependent equation</span>
      $$ i\hbar\,\partial_t\psi = -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\,\partial_x^2\,\psi + V(x)\psi $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Eigenstates &amp; Time-Independent Schrödinger</h4>
<p class="gloss">An <em class="term">eigenstate</em> of the Hamiltonian returns the same wavefunction times a constant (its energy); these states are stationary and simply oscillate in phase.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \hat{H}\psi_n = E_n\psi_n $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Stationary state</span>
      $$ \Psi(x,t) = \psi(x)\,e^{-iE t/\hbar} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Boundary Conditions at Interfaces</h4>
<p class="gloss">$\psi$ and its first derivative must be continuous wherever the potential is finite — required to keep probability current finite and avoid infinite kinetic energy.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \psi(x_0^-) = \psi(x_0^+),\qquad \left.\frac{d\psi}{dx}\right|_{x_0^-} = \left.\frac{d\psi}{dx}\right|_{x_0^+} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Normalization of Bound States</h4>
<p class="gloss">Total probability of finding the particle anywhere is one.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} |\psi(x)|^2\,dx = 1 $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Infinite well example</span>
      $$ \psi_n(x) = \sqrt{\tfrac{2}{L}}\,\sin\!\left(\tfrac{n\pi x}{L}\right) $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Reflection &amp; Transmission Coefficients</h4>
<p class="gloss">Probability fluxes for a wave hitting a step or barrier. They sum to one — every quantum is either reflected or transmitted.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ R = \left|\frac{B}{A}\right|^2,\qquad T = \frac{k_2}{k_1}\left|\frac{F}{A}\right|^2,\qquad R + T = 1 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Allowed Wavelengths in a Finite Volume</h4>
<p class="gloss">In a 1-D box of length $L$, the wavefunction must vanish at the walls, fixing $k$ and quantizing energy. This particle-in-a-box estimate is a useful approximation for any localized potential minimum.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ k_n L = n\pi \;\Longrightarrow\; \lambda_n = \frac{2L}{n} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ E_n = \frac{n^2\pi^2\hbar^2}{2mL^2} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">3-D box</span>
      $$ E_{n_x n_y n_z} = \frac{\pi^2\hbar^2}{2m}\!\left(\frac{n_x^2}{L_x^2}+\frac{n_y^2}{L_y^2}+\frac{n_z^2}{L_z^2}\right) $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Born Rule</h4>
<p class="gloss">Probability of an outcome equals the squared modulus of the amplitude — the link from wavefunction to measurement.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ P(x)\,dx = |\psi(x)|^2\,dx,\qquad P(a) = |\langle a | \psi \rangle|^2 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Quantization of Angular Momentum &amp; Spin</h4>
<p class="gloss">Orbital angular momentum is quantized in integer units of $\hbar$; spin is intrinsic and comes in half-integer ($\hbar/2$) units for fermions.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ L_z = m\hbar,\quad m \in \{-\ell,\dots,\ell\},\qquad L^2 = \hbar^2\,\ell(\ell+1) $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Spin-½ fermion</span>
      $$ S_z = \pm\tfrac{\hbar}{2} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Malus's Law for Photon Polarization</h4>
<p class="gloss">A single photon polarized at angle $\theta$ to a polarizer's axis passes with probability $\cos^2\theta$.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ I = I_0\cos^2\theta,\qquad P_{\text{pass}} = \cos^2\theta $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Projection onto a Basis</h4>
<p class="gloss">Any state can be expanded as a sum over basis states (e.g.\ horizontal/vertical polarization), with coefficients given by inner products.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ |\psi\rangle = \sum_n c_n |n\rangle,\qquad c_n = \langle n|\psi\rangle,\qquad P(n) = |c_n|^2 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Entangled States</h4>
<p class="gloss">A two-particle state that cannot be written as a product $|\phi_A\rangle|\chi_B\rangle$. Measurements on the two parts are correlated even when the parts are spatially separated.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Bell singlet</span>
      $$ |\Psi^-\rangle = \tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\big(|\!\uparrow\downarrow\rangle - |\!\downarrow\uparrow\rangle\big) $$
    </div>
</div>
<h3 class="subsection">Concepts</h3>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Quantization of amplitude — how particles arise</h4>
<p>A field's amplitude itself is quantized: instead of any value, the energy in each mode comes in discrete chunks $\hbar\omega$. Each chunk is a particle. "Particle" and "field excitation" are the same thing.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Interpretations of QM</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Copenhagen.</strong> Wavefunction collapses on measurement; quantum/classical boundary is real but unspecified.</li>
<li><strong>Many-worlds.</strong> No collapse — every outcome happens in a branch of the wavefunction.</li>
<li><strong>Spontaneous collapse (GRW etc.).</strong> Collapse is a real, random physical process happening at some rate.</li>
<li><strong>Bohmian / pilot wave.</strong> Particles have definite positions guided by the wavefunction; appears nonlocal and unpopular for relativity reasons.</li>
<li><strong>Positivism.</strong> Stop asking "what's really going on" — only predictions matter.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>EPR/Bell &amp; the death of local hidden variables</h4>
<p>Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen suggested entanglement implies hidden classical variables. Bell showed any <em class="term">local</em> hidden-variable theory must satisfy an inequality that QM (and experiment) violates. Reality is not locally realistic.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>The nonlocality problem</h4>
<p>Measuring one half of an entangled pair instantly fixes the joint state, regardless of separation. No usable signal is sent (the marginal distribution is unchanged), but the joint correlations cannot be reproduced by anything local.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Dirac's negative-energy sea &amp; antimatter</h4>
<p>The relativistic Dirac equation has both positive and negative energy solutions. Dirac proposed the negative-energy states are filled (the "sea"), and an absent state behaves like a positive-energy particle of opposite charge — a <em class="term">positron</em>. Generalizing: every fermion has an antiparticle.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>What spin is</h4>
<p>Intrinsic angular momentum that doesn't come from spatial motion — a particle has it even at rest. Comes in $\hbar/2$ units for fermions and integer $\hbar$ for bosons. Unlike orbital $L$, spin lives in an internal Hilbert space, but it adds to $L$ to give total $J$.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Bonding, antibonding, bands, and gaps</h4>
<p>Two atomic orbitals combine into a lower-energy <em class="term">bonding</em> state (constructive between nuclei) and a higher-energy <em class="term">antibonding</em> state (node between them). With $N$ atoms, you get $N$ closely-spaced levels — a <em class="term">band</em> — separated from the next band by a gap of forbidden energies.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Semiconductors: valence, conduction, holes</h4>
<p>The valence band is full; the conduction band is empty; the gap is small (~1 eV). Thermal excitation puts a few electrons into the conduction band and leaves <em class="term">holes</em> behind — missing electrons that act like positively charged carriers.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Metal vs semiconductor vs insulator</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Metal.</strong> Partially-filled band — electrons can move freely.</li>
<li><strong>Semiconductor.</strong> Filled valence band, small gap — conducts mildly when excited or doped.</li>
<li><strong>Insulator.</strong> Filled valence band, large gap — almost no carriers.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Doping, diodes, transistors</h4>
<p><strong>n-type</strong> doping adds electrons to the conduction band; <strong>p-type</strong> adds holes to the valence band. A <strong>diode</strong> is a p–n junction that conducts only one way (forward bias narrows the depletion region). A <strong>transistor</strong> uses a third terminal (gate or base) to modulate current flow between the other two — the foundation of every chip.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Fermi level &amp; Fermi pressure</h4>
<p>The <em class="term">Fermi level</em> is the energy of the highest occupied state at $T=0$ (or the chemical potential at finite $T$). Pauli exclusion forces electrons into ever-higher momentum states, generating a degeneracy pressure even at zero temperature — this is what keeps atoms from collapsing and makes solids solid.</p>
</div></div>
</section>
