<div class="legacy-content"><h3 class="subsection">Methods &amp; Math</h3>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Transition Laws: Pauli Exclusion &amp; Stimulated Emission</h4>
<p class="gloss">Quantum statistics modify ordinary transition rates: bosons "encourage" transitions into already-occupied states; fermions block them.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Boson rate (stimulation)</span>
      $$ W_{i\to f}^{\text{boson}} \propto (1 + n_f) $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Fermion rate (Pauli blocking)</span>
      $$ W_{i\to f}^{\text{fermion}} \propto (1 - n_f) $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Equilibrium Distributions from Detailed Balance</h4>
<p class="gloss">Setting forward and reverse rates equal at equilibrium ($\Gamma_{i\to j}P_i = \Gamma_{j\to i}P_j$) gives the occupation laws.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Fermi–Dirac</span>
      $$ \bar n_{\text{FD}}(E) = \frac{1}{e^{(E-\mu)/k_BT} + 1} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Bose–Einstein</span>
      $$ \bar n_{\text{BE}}(E) = \frac{1}{e^{(E-\mu)/k_BT} - 1} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Planck (photons, $\mu=0$)</span>
      $$ \bar n_\gamma(\omega) = \frac{1}{e^{\hbar\omega/k_BT} - 1} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Maxwell–Boltzmann (classical limit)</span>
      $$ \bar n_{\text{MB}}(E) = e^{-(E-\mu)/k_BT} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Density of States in $k$-Space</h4>
<p class="gloss">Going from a sum over discrete modes to an integral over continuous $\mathbf{k}$ requires the volume per state, $(2\pi/L)^3$ in 3-D.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \sum_{\mathbf{k}} \;\longrightarrow\; \frac{V}{(2\pi)^3}\int d^3k $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">3-D non-relativistic, single spin</span>
      $$ g(E)\,dE = \frac{V}{4\pi^2}\!\left(\frac{2m}{\hbar^2}\right)^{3/2}\!\sqrt{E}\,dE $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Photons (2 polarizations)</span>
      $$ g(\omega)\,d\omega = \frac{V\omega^2}{\pi^2 c^3}\,d\omega $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Change of Variables in 1-D Integrals</h4>
<p class="gloss">Useful for converting $k$-space integrals to energy integrals.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \int f(k)\,dk = \int f\!\left(k(E)\right)\,\frac{dk}{dE}\,dE $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Ideal Gas Law &amp; Equipartition</h4>
<p class="gloss">For a classical gas in equilibrium, each quadratic degree of freedom in the Hamiltonian carries average energy $\tfrac12 k_BT$.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ PV = N k_B T $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \langle E\rangle = \tfrac{f}{2}Nk_BT \quad (\text{$f$ active quadratic DOFs}) $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Working in eV Units</h4>
<p class="gloss">Useful order-of-magnitude scales for atomic and condensed-matter problems.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ 1\,\text{eV} = 1.602\times 10^{-19}\,\text{J} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ k_BT_{\text{room}} \approx \tfrac{1}{40}\,\text{eV},\qquad \hbar c \approx 197\,\text{eV·nm} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ m_e c^2 \approx 0.511\,\text{MeV} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Heat Capacity from Total Energy</h4>
<p class="gloss">Differentiate the total internal energy with respect to $T$.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ C_V = \left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Ideal gas / Dulong–Petit / photons</span>
      $$ C_V^{\text{gas}} = \tfrac{f}{2}Nk_B,\qquad C_V^{\text{solid}}\to 3Nk_B,\qquad C_V^{\text{photon}} \propto T^3 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Heat, Work, Entropy, Free Energy</h4>
<p class="gloss">First-law bookkeeping for an ideal gas: heat in minus work out equals change in internal energy. Entropy counts microstates; free energy is the energy "available" to do work at fixed $T$.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ dU = \delta Q - \delta W = T\,dS - P\,dV $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ S = k_B\ln\Omega = -k_B\sum_i p_i\ln p_i $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ F = U - TS,\qquad G = U + PV - TS $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Diffusion Equation &amp; Transport</h4>
<p class="gloss">A localized concentration spreads as a Gaussian whose width grows as $\sqrt{Dt}$ and whose peak height falls accordingly.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \frac{\partial n}{\partial t} = D\nabla^2 n $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">1-D point-source solution</span>
      $$ n(x,t) = \frac{N}{\sqrt{4\pi D t}}\,\exp\!\left(-\frac{x^2}{4Dt}\right) $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Microscopic relations</span>
      $$ D \sim \tfrac{1}{3}v\,\ell,\qquad \ell = \tfrac{1}{n\sigma},\qquad \kappa \sim \tfrac{1}{3}c_v\,v\,\ell $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Carnot Cycle Efficiency</h4>
<p class="gloss">The maximum efficiency of any heat engine operating between hot and cold reservoirs — set by the temperature ratio alone.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \eta_{\text{Carnot}} = 1 - \frac{T_C}{T_H} = \frac{W}{Q_H} $$
    </div>
</div>
<h3 class="subsection">Concepts</h3>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Irreversibility &amp; the H-theorem</h4>
<p>Boltzmann's H-theorem shows that a coarse-grained measure of disorder $H$ monotonically decreases (entropy increases) under collisional dynamics that erase phase information. Microscopic laws are reversible, but information about initial correlations becomes practically inaccessible — the arrow of time is statistical.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Poincaré recurrence</h4>
<p>Bounded systems eventually return arbitrarily close to any past configuration — but the recurrence time scales like $e^{S/k_B}$, astronomically longer than the age of the universe for any macroscopic object.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Entropy, free energy, temperature, chemical potential</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Entropy.</strong> $S = k_B\ln\Omega$ — log of accessible microstates; missing information about the microstate.</li>
<li><strong>Free energy.</strong> Energy minus $TS$; minimized at thermal equilibrium with a reservoir.</li>
<li><strong>Temperature.</strong> $1/T = \partial S/\partial U$ — how willing a system is to give up energy.</li>
<li><strong>Chemical potential.</strong> $\mu = -T\,\partial S/\partial N$ — the entropic cost of adding a particle.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Four laws of thermodynamics</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>0th.</strong> Systems in thermal equilibrium share a temperature.</li>
<li><strong>1st.</strong> $dU = \delta Q - \delta W$ — energy is conserved.</li>
<li><strong>2nd.</strong> $\Delta S \geq 0$ for an isolated system.</li>
<li><strong>3rd.</strong> $S\to 0$ as $T\to 0$ (non-degenerate ground state).</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Bose condensates, superconductors, lasers</h4>
<p>All three exploit the fact that many bosons want to occupy the same state. A <em class="term">BEC</em> is a macroscopic occupation of the ground state below a critical $T$. <em class="term">Superconductivity</em>: electrons pair into Cooper pairs (effective bosons) that condense, carrying current with zero resistance. A <em class="term">laser</em> uses stimulated emission to drive photons into a single coherent mode.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Maxwell's demon &amp; Landauer's principle</h4>
<p>A demon that sorts molecules to apparently violate the 2nd law actually stores information about each molecule. <em class="term">Landauer</em>: <strong class="key">erasing</strong> one bit of information dissipates at least $k_BT\ln 2$ of heat. Once you account for the demon's memory, the 2nd law is rescued — entropy and information are the same currency.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ \Delta Q_{\text{erase}} \geq k_B T \ln 2 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Spontaneous pattern formation &amp; detailed entropy increase</h4>
<p>Driven open systems (a heated fluid, a metabolizing cell) can develop ordered patterns that <em class="term">locally</em> reduce entropy — but they always export more entropy to their environment than they create order locally, so the universe's total entropy still rises.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Spontaneous symmetry breaking &amp; Goldstone/Higgs modes</h4>
<p>When a system's ground state has less symmetry than its Hamiltonian, one says symmetry is <em class="term">spontaneously broken</em>. Each broken continuous symmetry produces a <strong class="key">Goldstone boson</strong> — a massless mode along the symmetry direction (e.g.\ phonons, magnons). When a gauge symmetry is broken, the would-be Goldstone is "eaten" and the gauge boson acquires mass — the <strong class="key">Higgs mechanism</strong>.</p>
</div></div>
</section>
</div>
