<div class="legacy-content"><h3 class="subsection">Standard Model</h3>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Strong Force vs Electromagnetic</h4>
<p class="gloss">Both are gauge theories with vector mediators, but the strong force has <em class="term">three</em> color charges and self-interacting gluons — leading to confinement at long distances and asymptotic freedom at short.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Coupling running (sketch)</span>
      $$ \alpha_s(Q^2) \sim \frac{1}{\ln(Q^2/\Lambda_{\text{QCD}}^2)},\qquad \alpha_{\text{EM}}(Q^2) \approx \tfrac{1}{137} $$
    </div>
<ul style="margin-left:1.25rem;color:var(--ink-soft);margin-top:0.5rem;">
<li><strong>EM:</strong> one charge type, neutral photon, $1/r^2$ at all scales.</li>
<li><strong>Strong:</strong> three color charges, charged gluons (8 of them), force <em>grows</em> with separation → confinement.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Electroweak Unification</h4>
<p class="gloss">At energies above $\sim 100$ GeV, the weak and electromagnetic interactions are unified into a single $SU(2)\times U(1)$ gauge theory. The Higgs mechanism breaks this symmetry, giving the $W^\pm$ and $Z$ their masses while leaving the photon massless.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ M_W \approx 80\,\text{GeV},\qquad M_Z \approx 91\,\text{GeV},\qquad M_\gamma = 0 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Quarks, Leptons, Hadrons, Mesons, Baryons</h4>
<p class="gloss">The matter content of the Standard Model.</p>
<ul style="margin-left:1.25rem;color:var(--ink-soft);">
<li><strong>Quarks (6):</strong> $u,d,s,c,t,b$ — fractional charge, confined inside hadrons.</li>
<li><strong>Leptons (6):</strong> $e,\mu,\tau$ and their neutrinos — integer charge or neutral, no strong interaction.</li>
<li><strong>Hadrons:</strong> any quark composite.</li>
<li><strong>Baryons:</strong> three-quark states, fermions (e.g.\ proton $uud$, neutron $udd$).</li>
<li><strong>Mesons:</strong> quark–antiquark states, bosons (e.g.\ pion $u\bar d$).</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Mediating Bosons</h4>
<p class="gloss">A <em class="term">mediating boson</em> is the force-carrier exchanged between matter particles in a gauge theory. Four show up in the Standard Model:</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Force carriers</span>
      $$ \gamma\ (\text{EM}),\quad W^\pm,Z\ (\text{weak}),\quad g\times 8\ (\text{strong}),\quad H\ (\text{Higgs}) $$
    </div>
</div>
<h3 class="subsection">Gravity &amp; Cosmology</h3>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Einstein: Gravity is Curved Spacetime</h4>
<p class="gloss">The <em class="term">equivalence principle</em>: a small free-falling lab is locally indistinguishable from an inertial frame in flat space. Free particles follow geodesics — straightest possible paths — and what we call gravity is just acceleration in a curved geometry.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Einstein field equations</span>
      $$ G_{\mu\nu} + \Lambda g_{\mu\nu} = \frac{8\pi G}{c^4}\,T_{\mu\nu} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Geodesic equation</span>
      $$ \frac{d^2 x^\mu}{d\tau^2} + \Gamma^\mu_{\alpha\beta}\frac{dx^\alpha}{d\tau}\frac{dx^\beta}{d\tau} = 0 $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Hubble Expansion &amp; Cosmological Constant</h4>
<p class="gloss">Distant galaxies recede at speeds proportional to their distance — space itself is expanding. The cosmological constant $\Lambda$ in the field equations behaves like a uniform vacuum energy that <em class="term">accelerates</em> that expansion.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Hubble's law</span>
      $$ v = H_0\,d,\qquad H_0 \approx 70\,\text{km/s/Mpc} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Friedmann equation</span>
      $$ H^2 = \frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho - \frac{k c^2}{a^2} + \frac{\Lambda c^2}{3} $$
    </div>
<div class="eq-block">
<span class="label">Cosmological redshift</span>
      $$ 1 + z = \frac{a_0}{a_{\text{emit}}} $$
    </div>
</div>
<div class="topic">
<h4>Cosmic Microwave Background</h4>
<p class="gloss">When the universe cooled below $\sim 3000$ K (redshift $z\approx 1100$), free electrons combined with nuclei and photons decoupled. Those photons travel freely today as a near-perfect Planck spectrum at $T\approx 2.725$ K — direct evidence of a hot, dense early universe.</p>
<div class="eq-block">
      $$ T_{\text{CMB}} = 2.725\,\text{K},\qquad \frac{\Delta T}{T}\sim 10^{-5} $$
    </div>
</div>
<h3 class="subsection">Concepts</h3>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Evidence for the Big Bang</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hubble expansion.</strong> Galaxies recede with $v=H_0 d$.</li>
<li><strong>CMB.</strong> Near-perfect Planck spectrum at 2.725 K, predicted by hot Big Bang.</li>
<li><strong>Light-element abundances.</strong> Big-Bang nucleosynthesis predicts $\sim$25% He by mass — observed.</li>
<li><strong>Large-scale structure.</strong> Growth of structure from CMB anisotropies matches simulations.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Fine-tuning issues</h4>
<p>Several Standard Model and cosmological parameters seem strangely tuned: the cosmological constant is $\sim 10^{120}$ times smaller than naïve QFT estimates; the Higgs mass is much smaller than the Planck scale (the hierarchy problem); the proton-to-electron mass ratio sits where atoms can exist.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Entropy problem at the Big Bang &amp; the origin of life</h4>
<p>The early universe had astonishingly low entropy — the source of every subsequent thermodynamic arrow, including the negative-entropy gradients that biology exploits. Why initial entropy was so low remains a deep question about the universe's initial conditions.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Matter–antimatter asymmetry</h4>
<p>Equal early amounts of matter and antimatter would have annihilated into photons, but we observe a universe of matter. <em class="term">Sakharov conditions</em> for generating an asymmetry: baryon-number violation, C and CP violation, and departure from thermal equilibrium.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Anthropic coincidences</h4>
<p>If gravitational, nuclear, and electromagnetic strengths differed by small amounts, atoms, stars, or stable nuclei wouldn't exist. The "anthropic" response: in a multiverse with varying constants, observers find themselves only in viable regions. Whether this counts as explanation or evasion is contested.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Aesthetic issues — large free parameters</h4>
<p>The Standard Model has $\sim$19 free numerical parameters, and several ratios (proton mass / Planck mass, $\Lambda$ / Planck density, etc.) are huge dimensionless numbers with no agreed-upon explanation. A really fundamental theory is hoped to derive these rather than fit them.</p>
</div>
<div class="concept">
<h4>Pointers to beyond-Standard-Model physics</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cosmic acceleration.</strong> Type-Ia supernova data require dark energy / nonzero $\Lambda$.</li>
<li><strong>Dark matter.</strong> Galactic rotation curves and lensing demand non-luminous mass.</li>
<li><strong>Neutrino masses.</strong> Oscillations show neutrinos aren't massless — requires extension.</li>
<li><strong>Infinite divisibility.</strong> The fact that we can probe ever-shorter distances at higher energy without finding a "bottom" suggests new physics at every scale; quantum gravity hints something changes near the Planck length.</li>
</ul>
</div></div>
</section>
